---
scope: src/service/install/reference
alignment: unreviewed
description: "Plans the tool and hook reference scaffold — every shipped reference page copied byte-faithfully into the host's reference folder as its own step."
intent: >
  Plans the host-side install of the AIDE reference: one
  page per server tool and per hook event AIDE listens to, each set with
  its table of contents. Every page lands as its own step, byte-identical
  to what the package ships and kept in its own set, so host projects and
  the docs site read exactly the shipped reference. The page list comes
  from its single owner, never pinned here. Drift is reported per page,
  and the reference stays a category apart from the methodology docs.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every reference page the package ships is planned as its own step, byte-faithful with its source and kept in its set (tools or hooks). The installed reference reads exactly as shipped, with no rewriting or composition at install time.
    - The page list is read from the single owner of the canonical content set. This layer iterates whatever comes back rather than authoring or pinning the roster locally.
    - A page already present and identical is left alone; a drifted page surfaces as its own would-overwrite step. The consumer decides per page, and no page's status is rolled into a sibling's.
    - A single unreadable page fails its own step and leaves every other page's step intact.
    - The reference installs as a category of its own, apart from the methodology doc set, so either can be applied or skipped without touching the other.
  undesired:
    - A shipped page missing from the plan, or a planned page the package does not ship. The installed reference must match the shipped one page for page.
    - A page's content altered, its set flattened into a single folder, or an existing page overwritten without being reported first.
    - Pinning the reference roster inside this layer by list, count or filename. The roster belongs to its owner and grows as tools and hook events are added.
    - One page's read failure failing the whole category, or several pages folded into one step or one confirmation.
    - Reference pages planned under the methodology doc category, mixing the two sets in one plan or one install folder.
---
